## 2. Pennsylvania Surprise-Billing and Debt-Collection Law

### Act 146 of 2022

Pennsylvania enacted surprise-billing legislation in 2022 (Act 146). It applies to state-regulated, fully insured plans and affects which protections and dispute processes apply. **[VERIFY]** its current scope and effective provisions with the PID.

### Fair Credit Extension Uniformity Act (FCEUA), 73 P.S. § 2270.1 et seq.

- The FCEUA regulates debt-collection conduct and applies to **original creditors** (hospitals, physician practices, and in-house billing departments) as well as third-party collectors.
- It prohibits abusive, deceptive, and unfair collection practices, including collecting amounts the consumer does not owe.
- It requires accurate credit reporting, including reporting of disputed accounts.
- **Relevant sections:** 73 P.S. § 2270.4 (prohibited conduct) and § 2270.5 (remedies). **[VERIFY]** the exact subsection numbers for the conduct you are citing.

### Unfair Trade Practices and Consumer Protection Law (UTPCPL), 73 P.S. § 201-1 et seq.

- Under 73 P.S. § 2270.5, an FCEUA violation is treated as a UTPCPL violation.
- Under 73 P.S. § 201-9.2, a consumer may bring a private action for actual damages, and courts may award up to three times actual damages where the violation was willful, plus reasonable attorney fees. Treble damages are discretionary, not automatic.
- **Limitations period:** Pennsylvania courts have generally applied the six-year catch-all period (42 Pa.C.S. § 5527) to UTPCPL claims. **[VERIFY]** the current rule before relying on it.

### Federal FDCPA note

The federal Fair Debt Collection Practices Act (15 U.S.C. § 1692 et seq.) applies mainly to **third-party collection agencies and attorney collectors**. Its validation rights (§ 1692g) and credit-reporting rule (§ 1692e(8)) generally do not govern a hospital's or physician practice's own billing office. Cite the FDCPA only when a third-party collector is involved.

